00:13:09,300 --> 00:13:10,880
So, might there be some

270
00:13:10,880 --> 00:13:12,460
part of that that's not true?

271
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:13,480
Maybe.

272
00:13:13,480 --> 00:13:17,590
And there's also some popular

273
00:13:17,590 --> 00:13:21,700
myths about boot camps, for example,

274
00:13:21,700 --> 00:13:24,440
that there is a magic formula for success.

275
00:13:25,060 --> 00:13:27,680
And if you follow the path that we

276
00:13:27,680 --> 00:13:30,300
teach you, you can pretty well be guaranteed

277
00:13:30,300 --> 00:13:33,020
that you will be showered with money.

278
00:13:34,940 --> 00:13:37,020
And there are outfits out there

279
00:13:37,020 --> 00:13:39,100
that kind of halfway promise that.

280
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:40,500
Okay.

281
00:13:40,500 --> 00:13:42,100
So, I'm going to go ahead and get started.

282
00:13:42,100 --> 00:13:45,860
This left us with a moral dilemma.

283
00:13:46,740 --> 00:13:50,000
Do we hype it, promote the

284
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:53,260
myths, or do we tell you facts?

285
00:13:53,920 --> 00:13:59,160
We voted for facts because that famous professor,

286
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:02,620
Hadzima, articulated an equation for you guys.

287
00:14:03,800 --> 00:14:06,240
In your very first session,

288
00:14:06,240 --> 00:14:08,680
happiness is reality divided by expectations.

289
00:14:09,200 --> 00:14:10,630
Tonight, we're going to talk

290
00:14:10,630 --> 00:14:12,060
a little bit about expectations.

291
00:14:12,100 --> 00:14:13,980
Because the facts are, it's hard.

292
00:14:15,280 --> 00:14:16,720
And most startups fail.

293
00:14:17,860 --> 00:14:22,140
And most investors believe that you will probably fail.

294
00:14:22,540 --> 00:14:23,740
You may be brilliant.

295
00:14:23,920 --> 00:14:26,800
You may be charming and lovable, kind to animals.

296
00:14:27,280 --> 00:14:28,840
Investors think you're going to fail.

297
00:14:30,260 --> 00:14:31,840
There are lots of entrepreneurs.

298
00:14:32,540 --> 00:14:34,850
There's lots of competition for

299
00:14:34,850 --> 00:14:37,160
talent, funding, and other resources.

300
00:14:37,820 --> 00:14:41,500
And many of the entrepreneurs won't get the money.

301
00:14:42,100 --> 00:14:45,520
So, why am I talking about this?

302
00:14:45,660 --> 00:14:48,760
Well, when you decided to come

303
00:14:48,760 --> 00:14:51,860
here, or Harvard, or Tufts, or Northeastern,

304
00:14:51,860 --> 00:14:54,230
or Northwestern, or wherever you went,

305
00:14:54,230 --> 00:14:56,600
you probably knew walking in the door

306
00:14:56,960 --> 00:14:58,220
that you were going to take

307
00:14:58,220 --> 00:14:59,480
a beating somewhere along the way.

308
00:15:00,280 --> 00:15:02,090
And so, you probably didn't realize how much

309
00:15:02,090 --> 00:15:03,900
of a beating you were going to take.

310
00:15:03,900 --> 00:15:05,300
But you knew it was going to be hard.

311
00:15:06,440 --> 00:15:08,960
But there are a lot of people who promote entrepreneurship.

312
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:11,980
And to my, in my opinion, they over glamorize it.

313
00:15:12,100 --> 00:15:15,960
And they don't tell you how hard it is.

314
00:15:16,060 --> 00:15:18,960
So, in the spirit of disclosure, tonight I

315
00:15:18,960 --> 00:15:21,860
want to talk a little bit about the fact

316
00:15:21,860 --> 00:15:23,460
that even the smartest ones fail.

317
00:15:23,460 --> 00:15:26,160
And I know a lot of entrepreneurs.

318
00:15:26,160 --> 00:15:27,580
I don't know a single one

319
00:15:27,580 --> 00:15:29,000
who got everything right the first time.

320
00:15:29,560 --> 00:15:32,420
It's going to be important that you know,

321
00:15:32,420 --> 00:15:35,280
to this community question that you brought up,

322
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:37,340
you're not alone.

323
00:15:37,580 --> 00:15:39,800
And there are some resources you can draw on.

324
00:15:39,800 --> 00:15:40,940
And I'm going to talk a little

325
00:15:40,940 --> 00:15:42,080
bit about all of that this evening.

326
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:48,000
I do think that this is very important.

327
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:51,340
Entrepreneurs solve problems.

328
00:15:51,880 --> 00:15:53,100
They improve lives.

329
00:15:53,780 --> 00:15:56,650
Your new businesses drive growth, create

330
00:15:56,650 --> 00:15:59,520
jobs, and in some cases, create wealth.

331
00:16:00,220 --> 00:16:02,100
And that's a noble endeavor.

332
00:16:03,220 --> 00:16:03,880
But it's hard.

333
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:07,110
When I was interviewing some of my friends

334
00:16:07,110 --> 00:16:09,340
who are entrepreneurs for the book I wrote,

335
00:16:10,760 --> 00:16:12,100
one of the people who,

336
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:16,480
who's in the book, failed nine times in a

337
00:16:16,480 --> 00:16:20,860
row before she eventually hit it with number 10.

338
00:16:22,120 --> 00:16:24,720
So, that raises a couple of questions, of course.

339
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:26,390
How on earth do you

340
00:16:26,390 --> 00:16:28,060
come back after nine consecutive failures?

341
00:16:28,720 --> 00:16:31,470
And also, what did you do differently in

342
00:16:31,470 --> 00:16:34,220
number 10 that caused this one to succeed?

343
00:16:34,780 --> 00:16:36,600
Which sort of became the

344
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:38,420
paradigm for what I wrote about.

345
00:16:39,020 --> 00:16:42,060
So, tonight is less my session.

346
00:16:42,100 --> 00:16:44,060
Tonight is less about the practicum

347
00:16:44,060 --> 00:16:46,020
that you all said you liked.

348
00:16:47,620 --> 00:16:49,190
So, I may be making

349
00:16:49,190 --> 00:16:50,760
a mistake to deviate from that.

350
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:52,800
But I want to talk a little bit

351
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:54,600
about things you may need in the future.

352
00:16:56,260 --> 00:16:58,060
But first, a story that might

353
00:16:58,060 --> 00:16:59,860
cause you to think I accidentally walked

354
00:16:59,860 --> 00:17:02,960
into the wrong room, diving boards.

355
00:17:04,280 --> 00:17:06,380
Until a couple of years ago,

356
00:17:06,380 --> 00:17:08,480
I was a pretty serious springboard diver.

357
00:17:09,420 --> 00:17:10,759
Worked out with a coach

358
00:17:10,759 --> 00:17:12,099
and a small group of lunatics.

359
00:17:12,099 --> 00:17:13,760
At Boston University.

360
00:17:13,760 --> 00:17:17,020
And I was the guy up on the high board doing

361
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:20,280
stuff that nobody at my advanced stage ought to be doing.

362
00:17:21,919 --> 00:17:25,769
And one summer, I was screwing

363
00:17:25,769 --> 00:17:29,620
around at the outdoor pool in Lexington.

364
00:17:30,280 --> 00:17:33,600
My, at the time, 14-year-old daughter came up and said,

365
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:35,100
Dad, you know that thing you're

366
00:17:35,100 --> 00:17:36,600
doing with a coach at BU?

367
00:17:36,600 --> 00:17:37,719
Will they take kids?

368
00:17:38,820 --> 00:17:42,080
I said, I can probably make that happen.

369
00:17:42,100 --> 00:17:44,560
Do you want to learn how to do this stuff?

370
00:17:44,560 --> 00:17:45,670
Yeah, I want to learn

371
00:17:45,670 --> 00:17:46,780
how to do that flippy stuff.

372
00:17:46,780 --> 00:17:50,540
And I said, it's hard.

373
00:17:50,540 --> 00:17:51,340
It's terrifying.

374
00:17:51,340 --> 00:17:52,159
You sure you want to do it?

375
00:17:52,159 --> 00:17:56,340
Yep. So, I said, all right.

376
00:17:57,840 --> 00:17:58,860
And I signed her up.

377
00:18:00,860 --> 00:18:03,980
Because I knew that most 14-year-old

378
00:18:03,980 --> 00:18:07,100
girls didn't even want to be

379
00:18:07,100 --> 00:18:08,919
in the same building with their father.

380
00:18:09,419 --> 00:18:10,980
And I wasn't going to pass this up.

381
00:18:12,100 --> 00:18:16,570
So, I knew that there comes

382
00:18:16,570 --> 00:18:21,040
a point for most new divers,

383
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:23,690
you've learned how to work

384
00:18:23,690 --> 00:18:26,340
the board, you've gotten some altitude,

385
00:18:26,340 --> 00:18:29,160
you've learned a little bit of

386
00:18:29,160 --> 00:18:31,980
basic gymnastics moves, and then you flop.

387
00:18:31,980 --> 00:18:33,790
And I knew that she was

388
00:18:33,790 --> 00:18:35,600
going to get to that point.

389
00:18:35,600 --> 00:18:37,879
And sure enough, the coach realized

390
00:18:37,879 --> 00:18:40,159
she actually had some athletic ability,

391
00:18:40,159 --> 00:18:42,020
sent her up on the high board.

392
00:18:42,100 --> 00:18:44,550
I said, walk to the end of the

393
00:18:44,550 --> 00:18:47,000
board, turn around backwards so the water's behind you.

394
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:48,159
All right.

395
00:18:48,159 --> 00:18:50,599
And I want you to pull your knees

396
00:18:50,599 --> 00:18:53,040
up close to your chest, both of them,

397
00:18:53,659 --> 00:18:55,159
and balance on the balls of your feet.

398
00:18:56,100 --> 00:18:56,899
Water's behind you.

399
00:18:56,899 --> 00:18:59,399
And then I want you to fall over backwards.

400
00:19:00,139 --> 00:19:01,629
And when I say, hut, I want

401
00:19:01,629 --> 00:19:03,120
you to come out of the tuck,

402
00:19:03,379 --> 00:19:05,679
look back for the water, and go in head first.

403
00:19:08,120 --> 00:19:09,860
Now, the second time you do that,

404
00:19:09,860 --> 00:19:11,600
you think, well, what was the big deal?

405
00:19:11,600 --> 00:19:12,100
That wasn't that.

406
00:19:12,100 --> 00:19:13,639
That wasn't that hard.

407
00:19:13,639 --> 00:19:15,599
The first time you do it, you

408
00:19:15,599 --> 00:19:17,560
think, oh, my God, I'm going to die.

409
00:19:17,560 --> 00:19:20,320
And the last thing I will see is

410
00:19:20,320 --> 00:19:23,080
the ceiling of this pool just before I die.

411
00:19:23,080 --> 00:19:26,600
And sure enough, she rolled over backwards.

412
00:19:26,600 --> 00:19:28,350
And when the coach said, hut,

413
00:19:28,350 --> 00:19:30,100
instead of looking back for the water,

414
00:19:30,100 --> 00:19:33,749
she panicked and did a complete

415
00:19:33,749 --> 00:19:37,399
back flop from the high board.

416
00:19:37,399 --> 00:19:39,529
And all five of us, the

417
00:19:39,529 --> 00:19:41,659
rest of us in the class,

418
00:19:41,660 --> 00:19:43,740
knew exactly what that felt like.

419
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:46,830
She might have sunk three

420
00:19:46,830 --> 00:19:48,300
or four inches in the water.

421
00:19:48,440 --> 00:19:52,560
She just, tears, bright red back,

422
00:19:52,560 --> 00:19:56,680
came to the edge of the pool,

423
00:19:57,120 --> 00:19:58,980
and we all sort of comforted her for a minute.

424
00:19:59,660 --> 00:20:02,000
And then what about our workouts?

425
00:20:02,320 --> 00:20:03,960
Because I had explained to her in advance.

426
00:20:03,960 --> 00:20:06,460
I said, look, I'm not going to be

427
00:20:06,460 --> 00:20:08,960
one of those tiger dads or helicopter parents

428
00:20:08,960 --> 00:20:09,420
or whatever.

429
00:20:09,520 --> 00:20:10,280
You're going to do this

430
00:20:10,280 --> 00:20:11,040
because you want to do it.

431
00:20:11,660 --> 00:20:14,120
And PS, I need the coaching as well.

432
00:20:14,120 --> 00:20:15,330
So for that time that

433
00:20:15,330 --> 00:20:16,540
we were on the boards together,

434
00:20:16,540 --> 00:20:18,500
we're just fellow divers, OK?

435
00:20:18,500 --> 00:20:19,760
She said, deal.

436
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:23,180
So I knew she was at a crossroads.

437
00:20:23,180 --> 00:20:25,140
She was either going to decide, this is ridiculous.

438
00:20:25,140 --> 00:20:26,440
I've made a terrible mistake.

439
00:20:26,440 --> 00:20:27,320
That really hurt.

440
00:20:27,320 --> 00:20:29,760
I'm never doing this again.

441
00:20:29,760 --> 00:20:33,380
Or she'd change her mind.

442
00:20:33,380 --> 00:20:35,780
So I continued diving.

443
00:20:35,780 --> 00:20:38,570
After a couple of minutes, I saw her sitting there

444
00:20:38,570 --> 00:20:41,360
looking up at the platform where the two high boards were.

445
00:20:41,360 --> 00:20:44,590
And she got up and she walked over

446
00:20:44,590 --> 00:20:47,820
to the stairs up to the high board.

447
00:20:47,820 --> 00:20:48,639
I said, what are you doing?

448
00:20:48,639 --> 00:20:49,719
She said, I'm going to do it again.

449
00:20:49,719 --> 00:20:50,600
I'm going to get it right.

450
00:20:51,419 --> 00:20:51,820
Yes.

451
00:20:52,959 --> 00:20:54,459
Her decision, right?

452
00:20:55,399 --> 00:20:58,820
And actually, we continued diving for a couple of years.

453
00:20:58,820 --> 00:21:01,659
So she got to high school and discovered boys.

454
00:21:04,159 --> 00:21:07,259
Well, entrepreneurship is a little bit like that

455
00:21:07,259 --> 00:21:10,360
in that you're trying something you've not done before.

456
00:21:10,580 --> 00:21:11,360
And it's scary.

457
00:21:11,360 --> 00:21:14,659
And when you flop, it hurts, particularly

458
00:21:14,659 --> 00:21:17,959
if you've led a life of high achievement.

459
00:21:17,959 --> 00:21:19,500
It's painful.

460
00:21:19,500 --> 00:21:21,159
It's not something you're accustomed to.

461
00:21:21,159 --> 00:21:22,939
And you come to that crossroads.

462
00:21:22,939 --> 00:21:24,699
Now what?

463
00:21:24,699 --> 00:21:26,409
And a fair number of people

464
00:21:26,409 --> 00:21:28,120
say, well, this was a terrible mistake.

465
00:21:28,120 --> 00:21:31,500
I'm never doing it again.

466
00:21:31,500 --> 00:21:34,060
And that's the majority.

467
00:21:34,060 --> 00:21:36,719
And for them, it's the right decision.

468
00:21:36,719 --> 00:21:39,419
A few of you who have wiring defects.

469
00:21:39,419 --> 00:21:40,419
I'm not going to go into that.

470
00:21:40,419 --> 00:21:40,580
I'm going to go into that.

471
00:21:40,580 --> 00:21:44,620
Say, you know, that didn't kill me.

472
00:21:44,620 --> 00:21:46,460
I'm going to do it again.

473
00:21:46,460 --> 00:21:49,860
So think about it.

474
00:21:49,860 --> 00:21:52,560
If you're an innovator, what comes with innovation?

475
00:21:52,560 --> 00:21:53,699
Well, you're doing something new.

476
00:21:53,699 --> 00:21:56,300
You may not have done it before.

477
00:21:56,560 --> 00:21:58,060
Maybe nobody's done it before.

478
00:21:59,379 --> 00:22:01,709
There's no established roadmap,

479
00:22:01,709 --> 00:22:04,040
which raises the alarming possibility

480
00:22:04,040 --> 00:22:05,760
that you might not know what you're doing.

481
00:22:07,840 --> 00:22:08,800
Totally forgivable.

482
00:22:08,800 --> 00:22:09,800
Totally understandable.

483
00:22:09,800 --> 00:22:09,820
But you're not doing it.

484
00:22:09,820 --> 00:22:09,919
You're not doing it.

485
00:22:09,919 --> 00:22:09,980
You're not doing it.

486
00:22:09,980 --> 00:22:10,040
You're not doing it.

487
00:22:10,040 --> 00:22:10,220
You're not doing it.

488
00:22:10,220 --> 00:22:10,280
You're not doing it.

489
00:22:10,280 --> 00:22:10,379
You're not doing it.

490
00:22:10,379 --> 00:22:10,439
You're not doing it.

491
00:22:10,439 --> 00:22:10,540
You're not doing it.

492
00:22:10,540 --> 00:22:10,580
You're not doing it.

493
00:22:10,580 --> 00:22:10,639
You're not doing it.

494
00:22:10,639 --> 00:22:11,720
You're not doing it.

495
00:22:11,720 --> 00:22:14,520
Many people find uncertainty to be stressful.

496
00:22:14,520 --> 00:22:19,280
And you have to make decisions, a lot of decisions.

497
00:22:19,280 --> 00:22:22,419
And some of those decisions are going to be wrong

498
00:22:23,240 --> 00:22:24,819
because you're moving fast,

499
00:22:24,819 --> 00:22:26,399
you don't have enough information,

500
00:22:26,399 --> 00:22:28,040
and you got to make a decision.

501
00:22:28,659 --> 00:22:29,720
Do we raise the price?

502
00:22:30,080 --> 00:22:32,280
Do we sign a contract with the supplier?

503
00:22:32,659 --> 00:22:33,960
Do I hire this guy?

504
00:22:34,120 --> 00:22:35,360
Do I fire this one?

505
00:22:35,360 --> 00:22:39,040
So you have to ask yourself, can you accept that?

506
00:22:39,040 --> 00:22:40,540
And you have to ask yourself, can you accept that?

507
00:22:40,540 --> 00:22:43,849
Many people find that one of

508
00:22:43,849 --> 00:22:47,159
the appeals of being an entrepreneur is

509
00:22:47,159 --> 00:22:48,260
that you're your own boss.

510
00:22:48,780 --> 00:22:52,820
Is that true for any of you?

511
00:22:53,700 --> 00:22:54,420
Thinking about it?

512
00:22:54,420 --> 00:22:55,440
Yeah? Okay.

513
00:22:55,440 --> 00:22:56,580
Well, here's the good news.

514
00:22:57,100 --> 00:22:58,440
Nobody's going to tell you what to do.

515
00:22:59,560 --> 00:23:00,460
Here's the bad news.

516
00:23:01,159 --> 00:23:02,280
Nobody's going to tell you what to do.

517
00:23:04,280 --> 00:23:05,740
Right? You'll be in a situation you

518
00:23:05,740 --> 00:23:07,200
think, I don't know what to do.

519
00:23:07,200 --> 00:23:12,540
So let me tell you a couple

520
00:23:12,540 --> 00:23:17,880
